seg-5901f0b26939 = Telecom and power operators test a timing system by taking its GNSS away and watching what the clock does. Kshana does not do that for them, and does not touch their equipment. It reads the time-error record the operator's own instruments produced, splits it at the two times the operator states, and <b>measures each step against public ITU-T masks</b>. The scenario kind is <a1><c1/></a1>; the example scenarios are <c2/> and <c3/>.

# text
# c1: HARDWARE-SAFETY.md
seg-daf4b071bad0 = This is hardware-safety <b>stage { "1" }</b>: passive, receive-only, no RF from Kshana (<a1><c1/></a1>). Advisory software, not type-approved equipment. Nobody has rated, approved or reviewed this procedure, and it says nothing about whether a system meets a Recommendation.

# attr:alt
seg-b9429842db16 = A holdover test scored from a time-error record, from a real run of engine { "v0.39.0" } on scenarios/holdover-test-example.toml ({ "601" } samples every { "5" } s). GNSS is removed by the operator at { "600" } s and restored at { "2400" } s; the time error drifts to { "299" } ns at the end of holdover, past the { "100" } ns budget after { "675" } s, and returns inside the { "100" } ns band { "70" } s after restoration. Verdicts: baseline masks PASS, holdover masks FAIL, holdover envelope FAIL, recovery RECOVERED. Measured against the mask, not an approval. The times are the operator's statement, not verified by Kshana. Advisory: analysis output from a research and engineering tool, not type-approved navigation equipment and not a safety-of-life service.

