seg-0810b24c6f3e = The result is <b>measured against the mask</b>. The masks are transcribed from freely downloadable ITU-T Recommendations (the same tables as the <c1/> kind, listed in <a1><c2/></a1>), and every entry the Recommendations leave "for further study" is absent, not filled in. Kshana does not say that a clock or a system meets or is approved against a Recommendation, and the report does not either.

# text
seg-527189cf37c6 = <b>MODELLED.</b> The step logic, the line fit and the recovery rule are checked on records built from formulas whose answers are known before the run; no laboratory holdover has been scored against an external oracle, and no partner or laboratory has agreed to anything. A timing laboratory would be needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted.

# text
seg-ad8160fbed46 = <b>What is not covered</b>, stated in every report: whether GNSS really was removed and restored at the stated times; any step the operator did not record; masks defined through a { "0.1" } Hz filter for a record sampled slower than once a second; the instrument's own measurement uncertainty; and any Recommendation procedure beyond a time-error record (temperature profiles, packet-network load).

# text
seg-cf681db42f05 = <b>The recovery band is the test's own choice.</b> The default of { "100" } ns is the maximum absolute time error of a PRTC-A (ITU-T G.8272); it is a convenient line, not a requirement of any Recommendation for recovery.

# text
seg-4ce2f2052df4 = <b>No transmit path.</b> Kshana reads files. It does not generate a signal, and removing GNSS in a lab is done with the lab's own equipment and under its own authorisation.

